      en: "Xueshan, or Snow Mountain, is Taiwan's second-highest peak, standing at 3,886 meters within Shei-Pa National Park. It is world-renowned for its exceptionally well-preserved glacial landforms, particularly its large glacial cirques which date back to the last Pleistocene ice age. Cirque No. 1 is the largest and most iconic of these formations, providing a dramatic geological amphitheater near the summit. The mountain features distinct ecological zones, with the 'Black Forest'—a dense stand of ancient Taiwan firs—serving as a highlight for trekkers. Historically, during the Japanese colonial period, the mountain was named 'Tsugitakayama' (Second High Mountain). The standard route to the summit offers breathtaking views of the 'Holy Ridge,' a rugged and treacherous ridgeline connecting Xueshan to Mt. Dabajian. The area is a vital sanctuary for alpine biodiversity, including the endemic Formosan salamander. Climbing Xueshan is a popular pursuit, though it requires careful planning due to high-altitude weather and seasonal snow. Conservation efforts here are primarily focused on protecting its rare glacial relics and fragile alpine ecosystems.",
